# `actuarial::models::premium_curve`

The pure premium of each cell.

## Signature

```cpp
pluss::Series actuarial::models::premium_curve(const pluss::Series &incurred_losses, const pluss::Series &exposure);
```

## What It Does

Returns the pure premium of each cell, {math}`L_i/E_i`, one value per cell. A cell whose denominator is zero gets zero rather than an error, so a rating grid with empty cells stays usable; [`pure_premium`](pure_premium.md) is the aggregate over the whole book and is the figure to use for an overall rate. This is the first step of [`operators::price`](../pricing/price.md).

## Parameters

`incurred_losses`
: One-dimensional, finite series of losses, 0 or more.

`exposure`
: Series of the same length, finite and 0 or more.

## Returns

A series of the same length as the inputs.

## Errors

Throws `premium_curve: numerator must be >= 0` (and `must be defined`, `must be finite`) and the same for `exposure`, and `premium_curve: numerator and exposure must have same length`.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1: four cells

The figure for each cell.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const pluss::Series losses = pluss::make_series({520000.0, 690000.0, 810000.0, 1100000.0});
  const pluss::Series exposure = pluss::make_series({1000.0, 1200.0, 1500.0, 1800.0});
  const pluss::Series premium = pluss::make_series({700000.0, 820000.0, 1000000.0, 1230000.0});
  out.writeln(actuarial::models::premium_curve(losses, exposure).items<double>());
}
```

### Example 2: an empty cell

A zero denominator gives zero.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  out.writeln(actuarial::models::premium_curve(pluss::make_series({100.0, 50.0, 80.0}), pluss::make_series({10.0, 0.0, 16.0})).items<double>());
}
```

### Example 3: against the aggregate

The cells straddle the aggregate figure.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const pluss::Series losses = pluss::make_series({520000.0, 690000.0, 810000.0, 1100000.0});
  const pluss::Series exposure = pluss::make_series({1000.0, 1200.0, 1500.0, 1800.0});
  const pluss::Series premium = pluss::make_series({700000.0, 820000.0, 1000000.0, 1230000.0});
  out.writeln("cells:", actuarial::models::premium_curve(losses, exposure).items<double>());
  out.writeln("book: ", actuarial::models::pure_premium(losses, exposure).item<double>());
}
```

### Example 4: a grid of cells

Unlike the aggregate, the per-cell figure accepts any shape, so a two-dimensional grid of cells works; the two inputs must have the same number of cells.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const pluss::Series l = pluss::make_series({100.0, 50.0, 80.0, 30.0}, {2, 2});
  const pluss::Series d = pluss::make_series({10.0, 0.0, 16.0, 5.0}, {2, 2});
  out.writeln(actuarial::models::premium_curve(l, d).reshape({4}).items<double>());
}
```

### Example 5: the errors

A length mismatch and invalid values are refused.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const auto report = [&](const auto &call) {
    try {
      call();
    } catch (const std::exception &e) {
      std::string text(e.what());
      if (const auto cut = text.find("Triggered"); cut != std::string::npos) text.erase(cut);
      while (!text.empty() && text.back() == '\n') text.pop_back();
      out.writeln(text);
    }
  };
  report([&] { (void)actuarial::models::premium_curve(pluss::make_series({1.0, 2.0}), pluss::make_series({1.0, 2.0, 3.0})); });
  report([&] { (void)actuarial::models::premium_curve(pluss::make_series({-1.0, 2.0}), pluss::make_series({1.0, 2.0})); });
  report([&] { (void)actuarial::models::premium_curve(pluss::make_series({1.0, 2.0}), pluss::make_series({std::nan(""), 2.0})); });
}
```

